    "result_text": "All tests pass. \n\n## Summary\n\nI added email uniqueness enforcement to `POST /api/owners`, rejecting duplicates with **409**, following the existing telephone-uniqueness pattern exactly.\n\nNew cohesive units (no existing method changed except the YAML wiring):\n\n- **`CheckUniqueEmail`** \u2014 a new one-method function class. Reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, and if it has an email, compares its lower-cased value against every other owner's, throwing `DuplicateEmailException` on a match.\n- **`DuplicateEmailException`** \u2014 checked exception (extends `Exception`).\n- **`DuplicateEmailHandler`** \u2014 escalation handler responding `409 CONFLICT`.\n- **`DuplicateEmailException.yml`** \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `checkEmail` step between `checkTelephone` and `checkHousehold`.\n\n**Structural impact was kept minimal**: the only pre-existing function touched is the YAML pipeline (data, not code). All behaviour lives in brand-new small classes with `WMC_other = 0`, so no large class pays for the change. No existing Java method was modified.\n\nNote: `Owner.setEmail` already lower-cases the stored value, but `CheckUniqueEmail` also lower-cases both sides defensively so the \"lower-cased email\" rule holds regardless of storage. Email is optional, so a null email short-circuits without a false conflict.",
